            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



Air leakage is not a major issue for your home, but it is a "low hanging fruit" as far as improvements in comfort and energy use are concerned .The blower door test I performed on your house showed that it is constructed to be fairly tight from an air leakage perspective, which is excellent, however, I did identify a few locations that can be addressed:



** I believe the issue you are having with moisture on your cathedral ceilings, near to the large beams is related to air leakage through the ceiling. This is based on what was described to me during my visit, and from information provided about the relatively new age of the roof, and from images I took with the IR camera. Without being able to get up close to the exact spots where you are having an issue, makes this an assumption, but the assumption is also based on my investigation of your "attic space" where I pointed out places that your vapour barrier was not sealed. I suspect that air is leaking out around the fan, and around where the drywall/ wood beam meet. Considering this problem is most noticeable in the Spring, I believe that hot indoor air is condensing out in this general area, freezing, and then melting in early spring, or potentially in the Fall if large outdoor air temperature  fluctuations are occurring. One solution would be too use silicone caulking around the base of the fan, and along the seam of the drywall and the beam. If you do end up doing a siding/ insulation upgrade you could ask your contractor to pay particular attention to the exterior wall in this location and seal up any air barrier as needed.



** Another larger source of air leakage is the old combustion air pipe hole located beside the boiler in your mechanical room on the ground floor. Someone has stuffed pink fibreglass batt insulation into the hole, but pink fibreglass batt insulation is not a good air barrier. Since this penetration is no longer in use due to your newer direct vent boiler, I suggest filling the hole with spray foam, which will eliminate air flowing through it.



** Other penetrations in the mechanical room, such as pipe penetrations through the wall and floor can be sealed up with spray foam or caulking as required.



** Some of the older wood framed windows on the main floor are leaky around the frames and through the panes. The north facing picture windows in question are in need of replacement soon, as discussed at your home. See Window upgrade section for more details. To seal up any air leakage around the window frames you can remove the trim, and ensure low expansion spray foam has been applied along the edges. If completing a window upgrade, ensure the installers apply spray foam around the window after installation. 



** Other "slider" style windows in the home also allow air to leak through the slider mechanism. Slider windows are known to potentially have this problem, especially when considering the nature of shifting houses, and can be upgraded to hinge type windows when you choose to replace them. Again, please see the window section.



** The doors of your home also allowed air to come through around the weather stripping. Consider replacing weather stripping as needed, and ensure the latches are working as intended.



** Air leakage was not noticed through the floors of your home, and therefore the water seen under your home, near the utility corridors is likely the result of a plumbing leak. This merits further investigation from your contractor. Also noticed under your home was evidence of significant movement on the metal cribbing.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss, but it could result in a larger reduction. This is estimated to save you $130 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ. Although the savings aren't huge, the increase in comfort might be noticeable, for relatively low effort/ expense.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

MAIN WALLS



The main exterior walls of your home, for the purposes of our modelling, appear to be of 2x4 construction, at 16 inches on center, with R12 batts in the cavities. For the climate of any location in the NWT, and given the financial and environmental cost of heating northern homes, this is recognized to be insufficient. 



Current building practices suggest a minimum of R30 wall insulation for this climate. Since you mentioned that you are considering a siding upgrade on your home, I have modelled adding 4 additional inches (R20) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to 100% of your wall exterioir surfaces. Due to the large amount of labour involved in re-insulating and re-siding a home - and the high cost - this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.



Savings: By adding 4 inches (R20) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 1700 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 44 GJ, and qualify for up to a $4,000 rebate (if insulating 100% of the surface area) from the GNWT funded, Energy Efficiency Incentive Program administered by AEA. Please note that the rebate amount depends on the percentage of surface area re-insulated, and the R value of insulation added. Additionally, a minimum of 20% of the surface area must be insulated to qualify. If this were something you were interested in doing it would be best to discuss it over the phone in more detail.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate for insulation, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



*** There is an additional, specialized rebate program from Arctic Energy Alliance called  the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, accessble only until March 2024 that would allow you to double the rebate on the wall insulation. Please call me to let me know if you are seriously considering adding insulation to your home, so that we can discuss this program in more detail. ****



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.



This grant can be accessed in combination with any Arctic Energy Alliance grant.

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

CATHEDRAL CEILING INSULATION



The cathedral ceiling of your house appears to have about 8 inches (R28) fiberglass batt insulation, which similar to your wall insulation, is on the lower end. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20 ) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation.



As you mentioned to me during the energy evaluation of your home, your roof is quite new, so I consider this to be the upgrade of lowest priority for you. This would be something you do when it comes time to replace the roof far in the future. I included it in the report to give you an idea of the savings from such upgrades.



Savings: By adding 4 inches (R20) of rigid foam, expanded polystyrene insulation to the cathedral ceiling, you could potentially save $300 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.9 GJ.



There are no rebates availabel to help with a cathedral ceiling insulation upgrade .



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Exposed Floors



The exposed floor underneath your home appears to be 2x8 joists with 7 inches (R24) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation on the underside of the floor, just to give you an idea of what you can save. This upgrade would help with heat loss in your home, but is not a game changer. This would be a relatively straightforward, and comparatively affordable upgrade to complete, and provide added comfort and some savings.



Savings: By adding 4 inches (R20) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $ 300 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.9 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 250 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $350 is available for adding insulation to exposed floors.</foundation>
                <windows>

WINDOWS



The windows in this house (18 total between the two floors) are of various types. The windows in your 2nd floor living area are double paned, wood framed windows that are in need of replacement. The remainder of the windows in your home are double pane, vinyl frame windows, that slide to open, and could be upgraded, only if desired, or as needed. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all windows in your home with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. I recommend focussing your replacements on the 2nd floor living area windows an d completing full frame replacement to windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing all 18 windows with new ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 530 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 14 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, however there are rebates available from the Greener Homes Grant - Government of Canada. Please see their website for more details.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</windows>
                <doors>

DOORS



Both of the exterior doors in this house are steel doors with extruded polystyrene inside. I recommend you don't bother upgrading these doors (see savings below) as they are in good shape. Instead consider a cosmetic upgrade, such as paint, and replace weather stripping as needed. If you do decide to upgrade, choose ENERGY STAR?????? rated insulated doors for our climate zone, and by a door upgrade recommendation being included in your report you will qualify for the greener homes grant.



Savings: By replacing these 2 older doors with ones that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified for our climate zone, you could potentially save $ 20 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 0.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $125 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</doors>
                <WaterConservation>

WATER CONSERVATION



Your house has one older 13 liter toilet that should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush!



Savings: By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS





LED Lights ????????? Thermal Community - Inuvik



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 27.5 Pascals. When this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, but because your chimney vented appliances are direct-vent this is not an issue for your home. Your current natural gas boiler is not susceptible to back-drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - two range hood   

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. 



*** For your home you need to ensure the upstairs bathroom fan vent pipe is sealed and connected all the way to the outside. Your home is experiencing moisture issue and you need to expel that moisture to the outside. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor, which might be a good addition for your home. Installing a fan with a humidity sensor would cause the fan to turn on/off as needed to expel humidity from the house. You may also want to explore options for a different exterior wall vent hood configuration on the south side of your home, as it was mentioned that it freezes up in the winter. Ask your local contractor about options for this.



If you are cooking on your stove top you should also turn on the fan to rid your home of moisture from cooking.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Inuvik



The following December, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.73 per kWh

Oil - $1.73 per liter 

Natural Gas - $37.21 per GJ delivered

Wood - $341.25 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $14.70 per bag, $682.50 per tonne bulk delivered





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



